newer than the probe start, but selecting an external runtime root is outside
the
repository boundary. Linux BuildCookRun now passes `-UBADisableRemote` and a
target-local `-UBARootDir` below `.cache/build/run/` together with `-NoUBA`; the
runner fails closed if the repository UBA root is not supplied.

The bounded run was interrupted before accepting the 11,836-action rebuild and
the exact 130-byte LFS pointer was restored. A probe-harness stop hit the outer
`timeout` supervisor rather than the Python owner and briefly orphaned tagged
namespace descendants; the runner's exact managed-child token cleanup removed
all of them, leaving zero token survivors.

UE's Linux `RunUAT.sh` accepts `-nocompileuat`, which skips `BuildUAT.sh` and
uses the existing AutomationTool binaries. A real isolated Turnkey proof
completed in 3.57 seconds, rebuilt neither UBT nor AutomationTool, captured its
one engine-side write in the repository overlay, and left the external engine
unchanged. Linux UAT now always receives that flag.

The first repository-local UBA argument used embedded quotes, which caused UAT
to absorb `-skipdeploy -architecture=x64` into the UBA root string. The
corrected
argument is unquoted because the admitted repository path contains no spaces; a
real UBT log then showed all three flags separately and the exact repo-local UBA
root. That bounded run reached action 579 of 10,999 before its 600-second guard
restored the 130-byte map pointer and released the runner lock.

Two identical resumed Shipping runs then reused the stable makefile instead of
regenerating it, reducing the remaining agenda from 10,999 to 10,423 and then
9,028 actions. The second run exposed four retained ISPC generated headers whose
missing namespace trailer made Clang parse Chaos implementations below `ispc`;
the AST named the failing definition `ispc::Chaos::Collisions::GetTransform`.

The corresponding combined `*.ispc.generated.dummy.h` producer outputs were
also incomplete, while older per-ISA headers were balanced. Running the exact
multi-target header-generation response to completion succeeded in 19.973
seconds and produced a balanced 11,290-byte header. Invalidating only the four
incomplete dummy/copy pairs made UBT schedule `Generate Header` and `Copy`
actions, after which all four headers were balanced and the previously failing
`CollisionResolutionUtil.cpp` compiled successfully.

The runner now scans retained ISPC dummy headers before a non-validation build.
A dummy that opens the generated ISPC namespace but lacks its closing trailer is
removed so Unreal must regenerate the producer; an incomplete copied header is
removed with it. Complete generated headers are preserved, and redirected or
hard-linked candidates fail the existing real-file checks.

Two further identical bounded resumes confirmed that recovery does not
destabilize
the retained Shipping graph. The first started at 8,780 actions and completed
1,018 before its guard; the next reused the same makefile at 7,761 actions and
completed another 313. Neither run produced compiler errors, incomplete ISPC
headers, or writes to the user-level UBA cache.

Each fresh UAT overlay still recompiles its private `UE5Rules.dll` and
`UE5ProgramRules.dll` because the lower engine copies predate the current UBT
assembly. That isolated startup work did not regenerate the retained Shipping
makefile or prevent exact incremental reuse, so it is currently a performance
cost rather than a correctness blocker.

